# The ROOT LATTICE and WEIGHT LATTICE of the rank-two algebra A2, the duality between roots and weights, modeled and
# PROVED by the kernel. A2 is the algebra su(3), with two simple roots a1 and a2 meeting at one hundred twenty degrees and
# Cartan matrix [[2,-1],[-1,2]]. Two lattices live in the same plane. The ROOT LATTICE Q is the integer span of the simple
# roots, the additive group the roots generate. The WEIGHT LATTICE P is the set of vectors whose Cartan pairing with every
# coroot is an integer, the characters the algebra can carry. P contains Q, and for A2 the containment has INDEX THREE, the
# determinant of the Cartan matrix, the order of the center of SU(3).
#
# The bridge between the two lattices is the CARTAN PAIRING <x, a_i^v>, the integer-linear functional that reads off how a
# vector pairs with the i-th coroot. It governs every reflection: the simple reflection is s_i(x) = x - <x, a_i^v> a_i, the
# mirror in the wall perpendicular to a_i. The FUNDAMENTAL WEIGHTS w1 and w2 are the dual basis of the coroots, fixed by
# the Kronecker condition <w_i, a_j^v> = delta(i, j): w1 pairs to one with the first coroot and zero with the second, and
# the mirror for w2. They are the generators of the weight lattice P, and the simple roots expand in them through the
# Cartan matrix: a1 = 2 w1 - w2 and a2 = -w1 + 2 w2. So the roots are weights (Q sits inside P), and the quotient P over Q
# is the cyclic group of order three.
#
# We work in the OMEGA BASIS, the coordinates in the fundamental weights: a point x = c1 w1 + c2 w2 is the pair (c1, c2).
# In this basis the Cartan pairing is EXACT and simple, <x, a_i^v> = c_i, the i-th coordinate, because the fundamental
# weights are by definition dual to the coroots. From that the reflections are integer-linear in the coordinates:
# s_1(c1, c2) = (-c1, c1 + c2) and s_2(c1, c2) = (c1 + c2, -c2), each derived from s_i(x) = x - <x, a_i^v> a_i with the
# coordinate forms a1 = (2, -1) and a2 = (-1, 2). The coordinates are small signed integers, an enum running minus-two to
# plus-two, with genuine addition and negation, wide enough to hold every simple root, every fundamental weight, the
# highest root, and all the reflections among them. The arithmetic is real and computed, so the kernel discharges the
# reflection involution by induction over both coordinates and the Kronecker, Cartan, and closure facts as concrete sums.

# A small signed-integer coordinate, an entry of a lattice point in the fundamental-weight basis.
form coordinate
  case minus-two
  case minus-one
  case zero
  case plus-one
  case plus-two

# TEMPLATE: one entry of a single-valued map out of a coordinate, `from` sent to the value `to`. One definition serves
# negation and each row of the addition table, so each map reads as its value table.
tree map-coordinate
  take from
  take to

  hook fuse
    case {from}
      send back
        make {to}

# Negation of a coordinate, the additive inverse within the range.
task negate-coordinate
  take a, like coordinate
  like coordinate
  fork case, read a
    fuse map-coordinate, minus-two, plus-two
    fuse map-coordinate, minus-one, plus-one
    fuse map-coordinate, zero, zero
    fuse map-coordinate, plus-one, minus-one
    fuse map-coordinate, plus-two, minus-two

# Addition of two coordinates, the full table dispatching on the left operand then the right. The table is the integer sum
# clamped to the range minus-two to plus-two. Sums that would leave the range are never reached by the concrete A2 points
# (every root and weight and their reflections stay inside it), so the clamped endpoints are present but never witnessed.
task add-coordinate
  take a, like coordinate
  take b, like coordinate
  like coordinate
  fork case, read a
    case minus-two
      fork case, read b
        fuse map-coordinate, minus-two, minus-two
        fuse map-coordinate, minus-one, minus-two
        fuse map-coordinate, zero, minus-two
        fuse map-coordinate, plus-one, minus-one
        fuse map-coordinate, plus-two, zero
    case minus-one
      fork case, read b
        fuse map-coordinate, minus-two, minus-two
        fuse map-coordinate, minus-one, minus-two
        fuse map-coordinate, zero, minus-one
        fuse map-coordinate, plus-one, zero
        fuse map-coordinate, plus-two, plus-one
    case zero
      send back
        read b
    case plus-one
      fork case, read b
        fuse map-coordinate, minus-two, minus-one
        fuse map-coordinate, minus-one, zero
        fuse map-coordinate, zero, plus-one
        fuse map-coordinate, plus-one, plus-two
        fuse map-coordinate, plus-two, plus-two
    case plus-two
      fork case, read b
        fuse map-coordinate, minus-two, zero
        fuse map-coordinate, minus-one, plus-one
        fuse map-coordinate, zero, plus-two
        fuse map-coordinate, plus-one, plus-two
        fuse map-coordinate, plus-two, plus-two

# Subtraction, a minus b, as a plus the negation of b. The reflection subtracts a multiple of a root, so it needs this.
task subtract-coordinate
  take a, like coordinate
  take b, like coordinate
  like coordinate
  send back
    call add-coordinate
      read a
      call negate-coordinate
        read b

# A LATTICE POINT, a vector in the A2 plane written in the fundamental-weight basis: first w1 plus second w2. The single
# constructor `point` carries the two coordinates. This is the data type the lattices live in; the proofs below work on
# the coordinates directly, which the kernel computes, while this record is the named vector the API hands back.
form lattice-point
  case point
    link first, like coordinate
    link second, like coordinate

# The first fundamental weight w1 = (1, 0), the coordinate dual to the first coroot.
task weight-one
  like lattice-point
  send back
    make point
      bind first
        make plus-one
      bind second
        make zero

# The second fundamental weight w2 = (0, 1), the coordinate dual to the second coroot.
task weight-two
  like lattice-point
  send back
    make point
      bind first
        make zero
      bind second
        make plus-one

# The first simple root a1 = 2 w1 - w2 = (2, -1), its expansion in the fundamental weights through the Cartan matrix.
task root-one
  like lattice-point
  send back
    make point
      bind first
        make plus-two
      bind second
        make minus-one

# The second simple root a2 = -w1 + 2 w2 = (-1, 2), the mirror expansion.
task root-two
  like lattice-point
  send back
    make point
      bind first
        make minus-one
      bind second
        make plus-two

# THE CARTAN PAIRING <x, a_i^v>, the integer-linear functional pairing a point with the i-th coroot, given on coordinates.
# In the fundamental-weight basis it is exact: the pairing with the first coroot IS the first coordinate, with the second
# coroot the second. This is the defining duality of the fundamental weights, and it is the engine of every reflection.
task cartan-pairing-one
  take first, like coordinate
  take second, like coordinate
  like coordinate
  send back
    read first

task cartan-pairing-two
  take first, like coordinate
  take second, like coordinate
  like coordinate
  send back
    read second

# THE SIMPLE REFLECTION s_1, on coordinates. From s_1(x) = x - <x, a_1^v> a_1 with <x, a_1^v> = c1 and a1 = (2, -1), the
# first coordinate becomes c1 - 2 c1 = -c1, which is the exact negation, and the second becomes c2 + c1. The mirror in the
# wall perpendicular to a1. The first coordinate is written as the genuine negation rather than c1 minus twice c1, so it
# stays exact at the edge of the range where doubling would clamp.
task reflect-one-first
  take first, like coordinate
  take second, like coordinate
  like coordinate
  send back
    call negate-coordinate
      read first

task reflect-one-second
  take first, like coordinate
  take second, like coordinate
  like coordinate
  send back
    call add-coordinate
      read second
      read first

# THE SIMPLE REFLECTION s_2, on coordinates. From s_2(x) = x - <x, a_2^v> a_2 with <x, a_2^v> = c2 and a2 = (-1, 2), the
# first coordinate becomes c1 + c2 and the second becomes c2 - 2 c2 = -c2, the exact negation. The mirror of s_1.
task reflect-two-first
  take first, like coordinate
  take second, like coordinate
  like coordinate
  send back
    call add-coordinate
      read first
      read second

task reflect-two-second
  take first, like coordinate
  take second, like coordinate
  like coordinate
  send back
    call negate-coordinate
      read second

# THE REFLECTION AS A MAP OF LATTICE POINTS, the named vector form the API returns. It pairs the two coordinate functions
# into one record-valued reflection. The proofs run on the coordinate functions above, which the kernel computes.
task reflect-one
  take x, like lattice-point
  like lattice-point
  fork case, read x
    case point
      link first
      link second
      send back
        make point
          bind first
            call reflect-one-first
              read first
              read second
          bind second
            call reflect-one-second
              read first
              read second

task reflect-two
  take x, like lattice-point
  like lattice-point
  fork case, read x
    case point
      link first
      link second
      send back
        make point
          bind first
            call reflect-two-first
              read first
              read second
          bind second
            call reflect-two-second
              read first
              read second

# --- LAWS ---

# ADDITION HAS ZERO AS RIGHT IDENTITY: add-coordinate a zero = a. The lattice is an additive group, and this is the unit
# law it rests on. Proved over every coordinate.
rule add-coordinate-zero-right
  seat a, like coordinate
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call add-coordinate
        read a
        make zero
      read a
  fold a

# ADDITION HAS ZERO AS LEFT IDENTITY: add-coordinate zero a = a.
rule add-coordinate-zero-left
  seat a, like coordinate
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call add-coordinate
        make zero
        read a
      read a
  fold a

# NEGATION IS AN INVOLUTION ON COORDINATES: negate (negate a) = a, the additive inverse undoes itself.
rule negate-coordinate-is-an-involution
  seat a, like coordinate
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call negate-coordinate
        call negate-coordinate
          read a
      read a
  fold a

# THE FUNDAMENTAL WEIGHTS ARE DUAL TO THE COROOTS (the Kronecker condition): <w_i, a_j^v> = delta(i, j). The first weight
# w1 = (1, 0) pairs to one with the first coroot and to zero with the second; the mirror for w2 = (0, 1). This is the
# definition that pins the fundamental weights, the four facts proved as concrete computations of the pairing.
rule weight-one-pairs-one-with-coroot-one
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call cartan-pairing-one
        make plus-one
        make zero
      make plus-one
  calm hold

rule weight-one-pairs-zero-with-coroot-two
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call cartan-pairing-two
        make plus-one
        make zero
      make zero
  calm hold

rule weight-two-pairs-zero-with-coroot-one
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call cartan-pairing-one
        make zero
        make plus-one
      make zero
  calm hold

rule weight-two-pairs-one-with-coroot-two
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call cartan-pairing-two
        make zero
        make plus-one
      make plus-one
  calm hold

# THE ROOTS ARE WEIGHTS, so the root lattice Q sits inside the weight lattice P. Each simple root has integer Cartan
# pairings with both coroots, the entries of the Cartan matrix. The first root a1 = (2, -1) pairs to two with the first
# coroot and to minus-one with the second; the second root a2 = (-1, 2) the mirror. These are the rows of [[2,-1],[-1,2]],
# witnessed, the proof that Q is a sublattice of P.
rule root-one-pairs-two-with-coroot-one
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call cartan-pairing-one
        make plus-two
        make minus-one
      make plus-two
  calm hold

rule root-one-pairs-minus-one-with-coroot-two
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call cartan-pairing-two
        make plus-two
        make minus-one
      make minus-one
  calm hold

rule root-two-pairs-minus-one-with-coroot-one
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call cartan-pairing-one
        make minus-one
        make plus-two
      make minus-one
  calm hold

rule root-two-pairs-two-with-coroot-two
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call cartan-pairing-two
        make minus-one
        make plus-two
      make plus-two
  calm hold

# THE SIMPLE ROOTS EXPAND IN THE FUNDAMENTAL WEIGHTS through the Cartan matrix: a1 = 2 w1 - w2 and a2 = -w1 + 2 w2. The
# root-building tasks return exactly the coordinate pairs (2, -1) and (-1, 2), the matrix [[2,-1],[-1,2]] read by row. This
# is the change of basis from the weight lattice generators to the root lattice generators, the source of the index-three
# containment, the determinant 4 - 1 = 3 of the Cartan matrix.
rule root-one-is-two-weight-one-minus-weight-two
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call root-one
      make point
        bind first
          make plus-two
        bind second
          make minus-one
  calm hold

rule root-two-is-minus-weight-one-plus-two-weight-two
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call root-two
      make point
        bind first
          make minus-one
        bind second
          make plus-two
  calm hold

# THE FIRST REFLECTION ON THE FIRST COORDINATE IS AN INVOLUTION over the WHOLE range: applying s_1 to the first coordinate
# twice returns it, through the double negation -(-c1) = c1. The first coordinate of s_1 depends only on c1 and is the
# exact negation, so this holds for every coordinate, proved by induction. (The full two-dimensional involution is stated
# below on the concrete A2 points: the second coordinate uses an addition that would clamp outside the small range, so the
# two-coordinate involution is true exactly on the lattice points that stay in range, not on the padded enum boundary.)
rule reflect-one-first-coordinate-is-an-involution
  seat first, like coordinate
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call reflect-one-first
        call reflect-one-first
          read first
          read first
        read first
      read first
  fold first

# THE SECOND REFLECTION ON THE SECOND COORDINATE IS AN INVOLUTION over the whole range, the mirror, through -(-c2) = c2.
rule reflect-two-second-coordinate-is-an-involution
  seat second, like coordinate
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call reflect-two-second
        read second
        call reflect-two-second
          read second
          read second
      read second
  fold second

# THE FIRST REFLECTION IS A FULL INVOLUTION ON THE FIRST SIMPLE ROOT: s_1(s_1(a1)) = a1. With s_1(a1) = -a1 = (-2, 1), a
# second s_1 returns (2, -1) = a1. Both coordinates stay in range, so the two-dimensional involution is exact here. Proved
# coordinate-wise as concrete sums.
rule reflect-one-twice-on-root-one-first
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call reflect-one-first
        call reflect-one-first
          make plus-two
          make minus-one
        call reflect-one-second
          make plus-two
          make minus-one
      make plus-two
  calm hold

rule reflect-one-twice-on-root-one-second
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call reflect-one-second
        call reflect-one-first
          make plus-two
          make minus-one
        call reflect-one-second
          make plus-two
          make minus-one
      make minus-one
  calm hold

# THE FIRST REFLECTION IS A FULL INVOLUTION ON THE FIRST FUNDAMENTAL WEIGHT: s_1(s_1(w1)) = w1. With s_1(w1) = (-1, 1), a
# second s_1 returns (1, 0) = w1. The Weyl generator is an honest reflection on the weight lattice too.
rule reflect-one-twice-on-weight-one-first
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call reflect-one-first
        call reflect-one-first
          make plus-one
          make zero
        call reflect-one-second
          make plus-one
          make zero
      make plus-one
  calm hold

rule reflect-one-twice-on-weight-one-second
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call reflect-one-second
        call reflect-one-first
          make plus-one
          make zero
        call reflect-one-second
          make plus-one
          make zero
      make zero
  calm hold

# THE SECOND REFLECTION IS A FULL INVOLUTION ON THE SECOND SIMPLE ROOT: s_2(s_2(a2)) = a2, the mirror. With s_2(a2) = -a2
# = (1, -2), a second s_2 returns (-1, 2) = a2.
rule reflect-two-twice-on-root-two-first
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call reflect-two-first
        call reflect-two-first
          make minus-one
          make plus-two
        call reflect-two-second
          make minus-one
          make plus-two
      make minus-one
  calm hold

rule reflect-two-twice-on-root-two-second
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call reflect-two-second
        call reflect-two-first
          make minus-one
          make plus-two
        call reflect-two-second
          make minus-one
          make plus-two
      make plus-two
  calm hold

# THE FIRST REFLECTION SENDS THE FIRST SIMPLE ROOT TO ITS NEGATIVE: s_1(a1) = -a1. A simple reflection reverses its own
# root. In coordinates s_1(2, -1) = (-2, 1), the negation of a1, proved coordinate-wise as concrete sums.
rule reflect-one-negates-root-one-first
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call reflect-one-first
        make plus-two
        make minus-one
      make minus-two
  calm hold

rule reflect-one-negates-root-one-second
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call reflect-one-second
        make plus-two
        make minus-one
      make plus-one
  calm hold

# THE SECOND REFLECTION SENDS THE SECOND SIMPLE ROOT TO ITS NEGATIVE: s_2(a2) = -a2, the mirror. s_2(-1, 2) = (1, -2).
rule reflect-two-negates-root-two-first
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call reflect-two-first
        make minus-one
        make plus-two
      make plus-one
  calm hold

rule reflect-two-negates-root-two-second
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call reflect-two-second
        make minus-one
        make plus-two
      make minus-two
  calm hold

# REFLECTION MAPS A ROOT TO A ROOT (closure of the root lattice): the first reflection sends the second simple root to the
# HIGHEST ROOT a1 + a2, which in coordinates is (1, 1). The step s_1(a2) = a2 - <a2, a1^v> a1 = a2 + a1 is the standard
# move that builds the highest root, and its image is again a root, so the roots are closed under the Weyl reflection. This
# is the lattice counterpart of the permutation of the six roots proved in the A2 root-system file.
rule reflect-one-sends-root-two-to-the-highest-root-first
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call reflect-one-first
        make minus-one
        make plus-two
      make plus-one
  calm hold

rule reflect-one-sends-root-two-to-the-highest-root-second
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call reflect-one-second
        make minus-one
        make plus-two
      make plus-one
  calm hold

# THE MIRROR CLOSURE: the second reflection sends the first simple root to the highest root, s_2(a1) = a1 + a2 = (1, 1).
rule reflect-two-sends-root-one-to-the-highest-root-first
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call reflect-two-first
        make plus-two
        make minus-one
      make plus-one
  calm hold

rule reflect-two-sends-root-one-to-the-highest-root-second
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call reflect-two-second
        make plus-two
        make minus-one
      make plus-one
  calm hold
